## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 32-bit blocks? 
- Can you adapt to compute even parity per byte instead of odd parity?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of cross-limb bit indexing and non-trivial bit-twiddling without branching. It introduces a different angle: packing every other bit across a 128-bit value into a 64-bit result.

## Key Concepts

- **Piece-wise bit extraction with masks:** Isolate target bits using bitwise AND with appropriate masks.
- **In-limb and cross-limb mapping:** Map even bits within each 64-bit limb, then combine results.
- **Parallel bit-pack technique:** Use the classic "compress" trick—mask, shift, and merge repeatedly to collapse bits.
- **Endianness and limb numbering:** The `lo` limb contains bits 0-63, and `hi` contains bits 64-127 (little-endian representation).

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint64_t packEvenBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;              // keep even bits
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  return (hi << 32) | lo;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests familiarity with bit-slicing and fixed-mask patterns on multi-limb integers, not just 64-bit tricks. It also checks understanding of dividing work across 128-bit by treating halves independently. 

## Key Concepts
- Fixed masks for pairwise swaps (even/odd bits)
- 128-bit represented as two 64-bit lanes
- Branchless, constant-time bit permutation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x){
  const uint64_t A = 0x5555555555555555ULL; // even bits
  const uint64_t B = 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL; // odd bits
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & A) << 1) | ((x.lo & B) >> 1);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & A) << 1) | ((x.hi & B) >> 1);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to swap bits in 3-bit groups across 192-bit? 
- What changes if you must preserve performance across SIMD-like widths with additional limbs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend classic bit-trick patterns to wider word sizes without branches, a common need in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 128-bit data layout with two 64-bit halves
- Branchless bit counting and aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

int popcount128(uint128 a) {
  return (int)(popcount64(a.hi) + popcount64(a.lo));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIMD-enabled languages (e.g., intrinsics)?
- Compare branchless approach vs. lookup-based methods on large datasets.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design constant-time bit-manipulation without branches for 128-bit values, a common low-latency constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-reversal techniques
- 128-bit word handling
- Branchless bit tricks

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x=(x>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL | (x&0x5555555555555555ULL)<<1;
  x=(x>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL | (x&0x3333333333333333ULL)<<2;
  x=(x>>4)&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL | (x&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL)<<4;
  x=(x>>8)&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL | (x&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL)<<8;
  x=(x>>16)&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL | (x&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL)<<16;
  x=(x>>32)&0x00000000ffffffffULL | (x&0x00000000ffffffffULL)<<32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.lo); r.hi = reverse64(x.hi); uint64_t t=r.lo; r.lo=r.hi; r.hi=t; return r; }
unsigned isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x){ uint128 r = reverse128(x); return (x.hi==r.hi && x.lo==r.lo) ? 1 : 0; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to arbitrary width? 
- How does endianness affect input from memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise bit trick mastery and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bit hacks
- popcount and next permutation
- Undefined behavior on max value

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextBitPerm64(x) {
  const smallest = x & -x;
  const ripple = x + smallest;
  const ones = x ^ ripple;
  const shifted = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
  return ripple | shifted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 32-bit or arbitrary width?
- What are the performance implications on different CPUs?

